[{"title":"CSS Introduction","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=yfoY53QXEnI","notes":"<p>CSS stands for Cascading Style Sheets. CSS describes how HTML elements are to be displayed on screen, paper, or in other media. CSS saves a lot of work — it can control the layout of multiple web pages all at once. External stylesheets are stored in CSS files.</p>
<ul>
  <li>CSS = Cascading Style Sheets</li>
  <li>Describes how HTML elements should be displayed</li>
  <li>Can control the layout of multiple web pages at once</li>
  <li>External stylesheets are stored in .css files</li>
</ul>"},{"title":"CSS Syntax","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=yfoY53QXEnI","notes":"<p>A CSS rule consists of a <strong>selector</strong> and a <strong>declaration block</strong>.</p>
<ul>
  <li>The selector points to the HTML element you want to style.</li>
  <li>The declaration block contains one or more declarations separated by semicolons.</li>
  <li>Each declaration includes a CSS property name and a value, separated by a colon.</li>
  <li>Declaration blocks are surrounded by curly braces.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Code Example:</strong></p>
<pre><code>p {
  color: red;
  text-align: center;
}</code></pre>
<p><em>p</em> is the selector, <em>color</em> and <em>text-align</em> are properties, <em>red</em> and <em>center</em> are values.</p>"},{"title":"CSS Selectors","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=yfoY53QXEnI","notes":"<p>CSS selectors are used to "find" (or select) HTML elements you want to style. The five main categories:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Simple selectors</strong> — select by name, id, or class</li>
  <li><strong>Combinator selectors</strong> — select by relationship between elements</li>
  <li><strong>Pseudo-class selectors</strong> — select based on a certain state</li>
  <li><strong>Pseudo-element selectors</strong> — select and style part of an element</li>
  <li><strong>Attribute selectors</strong> — select based on an attribute or value</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Code Examples:</strong></p>
<pre><code>/* Element selector */
p { color: red; }

/* ID selector */
#para1 { text-align: center; }

/* Class selector */
.intro { font-size: 1.2em; }

/* Universal selector */
* { box-sizing: border-box; }

/* Grouping selector */
h1, h2, p { color: navy; }</code></pre>"},{"title":"How to Add CSS","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=yfoY53QXEnI","notes":"<p>There are three ways to insert a style sheet:</p>
<ol>
  <li><strong>External CSS</strong> — Link to an external .css file using the &lt;link&gt; element in the &lt;head&gt; section.</li>
  <li><strong>Internal CSS</strong> — Use the &lt;style&gt; element inside the &lt;head&gt; section of an HTML page.</li>
  <li><strong>Inline CSS</strong> — Use the style attribute directly on HTML elements.</li>
</ol>
<p><strong>External CSS Example:</strong></p>
<pre><code>&lt;head&gt;
  &lt;link rel="stylesheet" href="mystyle.css"&gt;
&lt;/head&gt;</code></pre>
<p><strong>Internal CSS Example:</strong></p>
<pre><code>&lt;head&gt;
  &lt;style&gt;
    body { background-color: linen; }
    h1 { color: maroon; }
  &lt;/style&gt;
&lt;/head&gt;</code></pre>
<p><strong>Inline CSS Example:</strong></p>
<pre><code>&lt;h1 style="color: blue; text-align: center;"&gt;Hello World&lt;/h1&gt;</code></pre>"},{"title":"Cascading Order & Multiple Style Sheets","url":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=yfoY53QXEnI","notes":"<p>When multiple styles are defined for the same element, CSS uses a cascading order. The priority from highest to lowest:</p>
<ol>
  <li><strong>Inline styles</strong> — inside an HTML element (highest priority)</li>
  <li><strong>External and internal style sheets</strong> — in the &lt;head&gt; section</li>
  <li><strong>Browser default</strong> — lowest priority</li>
</ol>
<p>If the same selector is defined in both an external and an internal style sheet, the one that is defined <strong>last</strong> in the HTML will be used.</p>
<p><strong>Code Example:</strong></p>
<pre><code>/* External CSS (mystyle.css) */
h1 { color: navy; }

/* Internal CSS (defined after external link) */
h1 { color: orange; }

/* Result: h1 will be orange because internal is last */</code></pre>
<p>An inline style has the highest priority and will override both external and internal styles.</p>"}]
